/S /P
/ActualText (There are two types of threads in Java: user threads and daemon threads.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 788 0 R
/ActualText (User threads are the threads that are created by the user or application. They are high-priority threads and the JVM will wait for any user thread to finish its task before terminating it.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 789 0 R
/ActualText (Daemon threads are the threads that are created to provide services to user threads. They are low-priority threads and are only needed while user threads are running. Once all user threads have finished their execution, the JVM will terminate even if there are daemon threads still running.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
